{"task_id": "Mbpp/223", "solution": "def is_majority(arr: list[int], n: int, element: int) -> bool:\n    # Helper function to find the first occurrence of an element using binary search.\n    # Returns the index of the first occurrence or -1 if the element is not found.\n    def _find_first_occurrence(array, length, target):\n        low = 0\n        high = length - 1\n        first_index = -1\n\n        while low <= high:\n            mid = low + (high - low) // 2\n            if array[mid] == target:\n                first_index = mid\n                high = mid - 1  # Continue searching in the left half for an earlier occurrence\n            elif array[mid] < target:\n                low = mid + 1   # Target is in the right half\n            else: # array[mid] > target\n                high = mid - 1  # Target is in the left half\n        return first_index\n\n    # Find the first occurrence of the element.\n    first_index = _find_first_occurrence(arr, n, element)\n\n    # If the element is not found, it cannot be a majority element.\n    if first_index == -1:\n        return False\n\n    # Check if the element also appears at the index that would ensure it occurs\n    # more than n/2 times. This index is (first_occurrence + n//2).\n    # If arr[first_index + n//2] is the same element, then there are at least\n    # (n//2 + 1) occurrences, satisfying the majority condition.\n    required_count_index = first_index + n // 2\n\n    # Verify that this calculated index is within bounds and contains the target element.\n    if required_count_index < n and arr[required_count_index] == element:\n        return True\n    else:\n        return False"}
