{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "def _count_sequences_recursive(current_length: int, last_element: int, m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Recursive helper to count valid sequences.\n    It builds sequences from `last_element` until `n` length, respecting rules.\n    \"\"\"\n    if current_length == n:\n        return 1\n\n    count = 0\n    min_next_element = 2 * last_element\n\n    for next_element in range(min_next_element, m + 1):\n        # Optimization: Prune branches where subsequent elements would exceed m.\n        if n - current_length - 1 >= 0 and next_element > m // (2**(n - current_length - 1)):\n            break\n\n        count += _count_sequences_recursive(current_length + 1, next_element, m, n)\n    return count\n\ndef get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total number of possible sequences of length n.\n    Each element is a positive integer, greater than or equal to twice\n    the previous element, and less than or equal to m.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return m\n\n    total_count = 0\n    # Calculate the maximum possible value for the first element.\n    # This ensures a sequence of length n can be formed without exceeding m.\n    max_first_element = m // (2**(n - 1)) if (n - 1) >= 0 else m\n    if n == 1:\n        max_first_element = m\n\n    for first_element in range(1, max_first_element + 1):\n        total_count += _count_sequences_recursive(1, first_element, m, n)\n\n    return total_count"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/244", "solution": "def next_Perfect_Square(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Finds the smallest perfect square strictly greater than the given number.\"\"\"\n    # Calculate the integer part of the square root of the input number.\n    root = int(number**0.5)\n    # The root of the next perfect square will be one greater than this.\n    next_root = root + 1\n    # Square the next root to get the next perfect square.\n    return next_root**2"}
