{"task_id": "Mbpp/256", "solution": "def count_Primes_nums(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of prime numbers strictly less than the given non-negative integer.\n    Uses a trial division method for primality test for each candidate number.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number <= 2:\n        return 0\n\n    prime_count = 0\n    for i in range(2, number):\n        is_prime = True\n        # Check for divisors from 2 up to the square root of i\n        for j in range(2, int(i**0.5) + 1):\n            if i % j == 0:\n                is_prime = False\n                break\n        if is_prime:\n            prime_count += 1\n            \n    return prime_count"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/247", "solution": "def lps(input_string: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the length of the longest palindromic subsequence in the given string.\n    This uses a dynamic programming approach.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(input_string)\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # dp[i][j] stores the length of the longest palindromic subsequence\n    # of the substring input_string[i...j]\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n\n    # All single characters are palindromes of length 1\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i][i] = 1\n\n    # Iterate for the length of the substring (from 2 to n)\n    for length in range(2, n + 1):\n        # Iterate for the starting index i of the substring\n        for i in range(n - length + 1):\n            j = i + length - 1  # Calculate the ending index j\n\n            if input_string[i] == input_string[j]:\n                # If the characters at the ends match, they can form part of the palindrome.\n                # Add 2 (for input_string[i] and input_string[j]) plus the LPS of the inner substring.\n                # If length is 2, dp[i+1][j-1] corresponds to an empty string (length 0), so it adds 0.\n                dp[i][j] = 2 + (dp[i + 1][j - 1] if length > 2 else 0)\n            else:\n                # If characters don't match, take the maximum of:\n                # 1. LPS of input_string[i+1...j] (excluding input_string[i])\n                # 2. LPS of input_string[i...j-1] (excluding input_string[j])\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i + 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])\n\n    # The result for the entire string is stored at dp[0][n-1]\n    return dp[0][n - 1]"}
