{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Sets the left-most (most significant) unset bit of an integer.\n    For example, set_left_most_unset_bit(10) (binary 1010) returns 14 (binary 1110).\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        return n # The design context mentioned raising ValueError for negative numbers, but the requirement is to not raise exceptions for edge cases. Returning original value as behavior for negative numbers is not defined by example.\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # Iterate from the bit position just to the left of the MSB, downwards to 0.\n    # This identifies the \"left-most\" 0-bit within the current active bit range of `n`.\n    for i in range(n.bit_length() - 1, -1, -1):\n        if not (n & (1 << i)):  # If the i-th bit is 0\n            return n | (1 << i) # Set the i-th bit and return.\n\n    # If the loop finishes, it means all bits up to n.bit_length() - 1 are set.\n    # In this case, the left-most unset bit is at position n.bit_length().\n    # For example, if n=7 (0b111), bit_length() is 3. The unset bit is at position 3, making it 0b1111 (15).\n    return n | (1 << n.bit_length())"}
